> Ad platforms and marketplaces show bidders a bid landscape, the estimated chance of winning at each candidate bid, and leave the choice to them or to their agents, which increasingly run on reasoning language models. We find that small reasoning models mostly get the arithmetic right and the bid wrong. Six open models of up to 14B parameters choose among candidate bids with win curves from eBay prices, under fixed token budgets, and are scored on held-out auctions against an exact calculator. They recover 42--85\% of the calculator's lead. Most of the rest is lost after the trace has already written the best option's value as the largest. The Qwen models run out of tokens before finishing the comparison; Phi-mini and Granite-8B finish it and then answer against it in 40--50\% of finished traces, following list position. Wrong answers lean toward the bid most likely to win, which costs bidders under pay-your-bid pricing but not under eBay's own rule. Sending answers that disagree with the trace's own numbers to a calculator recovers 73--93\% of the gap on held-out products.
